WEBVTT

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.400
Encounter

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.670
Shippouen

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.040
Sensei! Here you go.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.640
Thank you.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.480
Now that we all have drinks,
let's raise our glasses!

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:16.680
Hold on.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.650
We need to introduce everyone first.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.390
You're right.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.720
Let me introduce myself again.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.590
I'm the chief of the dictionary editorial
department, Araki.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.530
I will be leaving work to retire, but I'll be
sure to pop by as much as I can.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.430
We're going to miss him.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.700
Nishioka is... well, you can see how he is.

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.340
That's it?!

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.270
And over there is Sasaki-san.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.380
She's a contract worker mostly in charge
of organizing and sorting documents.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.740
I'm Sasaki.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:46.450
I'm Majime.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.420
And this is Matsumoto-sensei.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.620
He supervises most of the dictionaries
we publish.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.220
I'm Matsumoto.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.220
Pleased to meet you.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.390
I'm Majime.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.130
The pleasure is mine.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.260
Majime, loosen up a little!

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.560
This is your welcoming party,
so stop being so nervous.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.230
Yes, I'm sorry.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.670
What is it?

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.840
Oh, it's nothing. It's just...

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.480
Oh, this?

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.180
It's my vocabulary list.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.520
Whenever I find a new word or meaning,
I write it down in this list.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.320
I've been doing it for years now.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.220
I can't leave without it.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.310
Great Passage

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:54.650
A quiet dreamlike morning, the madder red sky
shines through the window and reflects on my pen

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.790
The vivid clatter of the night,
I wander between cities like a cat

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:07.430
Deep thoughts, flexible conception;
foreseeable aspirations, distant memories; unruly hair

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:13.870
Narrow mind, large circle of friends;
long silence, short greeting;tone of voice

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:20.370
The light in our eyes that differ from each other,
traces of friendship that’s not in the treaty

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:26.680
In the ocean that sweeps the sand of eternity

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:33.650
Swept and sunk in the waves with sparse
opposites and similarities

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:40.160
Clashing and exact opposites become
resolved and connected

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:46.700
Reference, conclusion; I’ll gather words that have
scattered over many months and years

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:53.170
The fragrant sea breeze;
the connection that rests on the seashore, become one.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.450
Anyway, I bet you're disappointed
to transfer

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.520
to the dictionary editorial
department.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.890
It doesn't have a very glamorous image.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.430
I mean, not that it's not totally justified.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.860
Oh, I'm not disappointed.
I like dictionaries.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.200
Majime-san?

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.800
What are you doing?!

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.270
It's because you started talking to him.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.910
Wait, it's my fault?

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:27.970
I'm so sorry.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.310
-Did you get any on you?
-No.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:33.480
I'm so sorry.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.650
How is he going to get any work done
like that?

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.530
I heard you majored in linguistics
at university.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.830
Were you always interested in words?

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.430
Yes, but it was all just information.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.370
I was really interested in the word
"dog" as a kid.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.370
-Dog? You mean the animal.
-Yes.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.740
Dogs have been mankind's faithful
companion since ancient times.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:05.710
But what about the phrases "military dog"
or "die like a dog"?

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:10.580
You're asking me?

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.020
Honestly, what am I going to do with you?

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.720
What about you, Majime?

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.900
A military dog is a spy.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.670
Die like a dog would mean
to die in vain or a waste.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.270
The word dog

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.370
is often figuratively used
in a negative connotation, I believe.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:37.840
The word dog has many meanings
and conjure as many images.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:43.050
I was so fascinated, I became obsessed
with words.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:46.990
Araki-san, Araki-san,

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.820
could we not talk about words today?

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.290
Oh, hey, do you have a girlfriend, Majime?

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:54.660
No.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.560
Then I'll set you up on a group date.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.070
Give me your cell phone number.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.730
Hey, Nishioka!

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.440
I don't have one.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:04.840
What, why?!

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.980
I returned the company phone I had been
using for my sales position.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.510
Isn't that inconvenient?

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:11.880
No, not especially.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.750
Then how do you usually stay in contact?

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.650
There is nobody I need to contact
on a regular basis,

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.420
and there is a communal phone
at my lodge.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.960
Is that really the issue here?

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.890
Then how about your hobbies?

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.600
Hobbies?

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.700
If I had to say,

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.630
it would be observing people
getting on escalators.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.070
What?

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.840
Is that fun?

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.110
Yes.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.980
During my commute, whenever I get off
the train and onto the platform,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.910
I purposefully walk slow.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.980
The other passengers walk past me

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:49.920
and rush towards the escalator.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:55.620
The people naturally converge into two
lines as if they were being controlled.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.760
It's patient and systematic.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:03.400
It's a beautiful sight that makes me not
even mind the morning rush.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:09.900
Araki...

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:11.870
Yes.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.110
Majime.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:20.110
Do you know why we're trying to create
a new dictionary?

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:22.820
No.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:26.920
A dictionary is a ship that navigates
an ocean of words.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.920
Without words, you cannot
express your thoughts

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.030
or be able to have any sort of
deep understanding of others.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.500
People board the ship we call dictionaries
and find the perfect words

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.170
to gather the small lights floating
to the top of the dark waters.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.610
Words are lights.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.140
But in the ever-changing world,

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.010
unable to find the right words,

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.850
there are those who lead troubled lives

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.450
caged with their own trapped feelings.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.650
We need a ship people like that
can feel safe boarding.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.430
That is the dictionary
we are trying to create.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.530
Made up with the kanji characters
meaning "to cross the vast Ocean",

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.630
it's pronounced "Daitokai."

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:16.540
We are creating a ship to cross
the vast ocean of words.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:19.670
Proactively inclusive of new words

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:23.210
with clear and concise interpretations.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:29.120
All in order to make Daitokai a dictionary
that brings people together.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.450
One of Matsumoto-sensei's faults
is that his stories tend to run long.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:36.460
To sum it up, what's a dictionary?

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:40.230
How many years have you been with
the dictionary editorial department?

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.130
Let me think...

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.060
To sum it up,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.840
a tool that can help assist everyone
to understand one another.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.170
Well, that's pretty lofty.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:56.610
Would you help me create it?

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.750
All right! On that note, how about
we raise our glasses again!

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.820
Honestly, you're so hopeless...

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.220
I think it's nice.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.220
Not to be presumptuous,

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.930
but I will take the lead here.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.570
To the dictionary
editorial department's voyage!

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.070
Cheers!

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.970
Cheers.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:30.740
-Majime, use the desk next to Nishioka.
-Okay.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.550
Listen carefully, dictionary making starts
by gathering words.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.250
Everyday conversations, billboards,

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.520
tonight's TV show or radio program,

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:41.920
I want you to gather the words
from everywhere

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:45.520
and write them down in this vocabulary
list with examples.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.530
Okay.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:48.790
Is there anything else?

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.530
First, I'll have you select some entry
words.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.130
Kojien, Daijirin, and Daijisen
are the big three

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.300
medium-sized dictionaries
like Daitokai.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.570
If the word is listed in all three,
it gets two circles.

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:02.680
If it's listed in two of them,
it's a single circle.

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.880
If it's only listen in one,
mark it with a triangle.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:08.980
Mark them down in the corner
of the vocabulary list.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.750
What are the marks for?

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.920
Of the words we've gathered in our lists,

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:17.220
we want to see what words we'd like
as entry words for Daitokai.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:21.960
Currently, we're planning
around 230,000 words for Daitokai.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:25.000
Two circles mean we will use it,
barring something significant.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.640
As you go down from circle to triangle,
it becomes less likely to be included.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:32.040
But the real issue are the words
with no markings

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.070
that aren't in any
of the dictionaries.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:39.810
Daitokai will change depending on which
of those words we choose to use.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:43.850
But this work takes forever.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.090
It's because you always take breaks.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:50.760
No, way! I mean,
look at all of these words.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.690
Will we actually get Daitokai finished?

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:56.660
It'll take at least ten years.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:57.830
Ten years?!

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:58.970
vocabulary list

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:01.200
I wonder what am I going to be doing
in ten years?

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:03.700
Making this dictionary that whole time.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.100
What?!

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:06.740
How sad's my life?

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.010
I'll do my best!

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.810
What? Why?!

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:18.720
That over there is the kitchen
and the bathroom is right next to it.

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:21.250
Sasaki-san keeps it clean for us,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:24.060
but try to tidy up when you can.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:25.830
Okay.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.790
It's an old building, isn't it?

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.330
Yes. I mean, no.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:38.300
It may look like the dictionary editorial
department was relegated to the corner.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.740
But this was the main building
around the time I first started here.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.410
The company also put in effort
to making dictionaries.

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:47.850
So we weren't relegated, we remained.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.080
That's why we have to leave
a dictionary behind as well.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.220
dictionary editorial
department

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:54.120
Although, this is just
my own personal reasoning.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:55.990
I didn't...

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.960
Now, all that's left is the reference room.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.110
These are the words

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:16.910
Matsumoto-sensei and the dictionary
editorial department have collected.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.110
There are over 900,000 words here.

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:20.710
And it'll keep growing.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.220
We will select from among them

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.790
the words we will add to Daitokai

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.190
and compress them into a limited
80mm thickness.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.960
And it will be 230,000 words
from the entry words alone...

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.090
That's right.

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:37.200
What's more, we have to add
interpretations to each one.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:39.130
It's real work.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:43.770
Hey, Majime.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.940
I don't believe that dictionaries
are necessarily the be-all, end-all.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:50.780
What do you mean?

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.880
I just mean that they're made by people.

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:57.220
You can't gather every Japanese word.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:59.850
And words are alive,

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:03.290
their uses and nuances
change with the times.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:07.290
When you think of it that way, you could
say there's no correct interpretation.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:08.460
Even so,

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.430
those who make dictionaries
continue to try.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.500
At a glance, dictionaries may seem
like a cold list of letters,

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.040
but actually, it took a lot of thought
and care to make.

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:25.950
That's amazing.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:28.310
Majime.

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:32.820
Please give Daitokai your passion.

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:37.290
I know you must still be adjusting
to your new environment.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:40.030
But if I could,

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:43.030
if there's anything,

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.260
something even I could do

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:49.030
to help create this dictionary...

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:55.140
When I heard your escalator story
at your welcoming party,

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:57.810
I knew it right then.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.010
You are suitable for making dictionaries.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:05.350
The way these countless words are
orderly collected into a single dictionary

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.520
is similar to the people
in your escalator story.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:27.070
Majime.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.380
Make a good dictionary.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:36.180
A ship that can carry a great number
of people with peace of mind.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:40.990
And a ship that only you could build...

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:57.210
The train now approaching platform four
is a local line to Nishi-Takashimadaira.

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:08.190
The train is now approaching
platform four.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:09.860
Please step back behind the white line...

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.860
A ship only I can build...

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:19.270
A ship that can carry a great number
of people with peace of mind.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:28.980
Oh, welcome back, Micchan.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.880
I'm home.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.510
You seem a little down.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.680
Oh, it's nothing like that...

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:46.730
We're already at the harvest moon
this year.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.900
I really hate how fast time passes
as you age.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.200
I didn't even remember until I saw
the grass and dumplings.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:57.670
The moon still looks the same to me.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.940
Your food is always so good, Take-san.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:04.940
I didn't know you knew how to flatter!

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:06.710
It's not flattery.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.920
Did it really seem that I was dispirited?

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.290
It does.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.120
Is work hard?

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:18.960
No...

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:22.560
I actually transferred to the dictionary
editorial department.

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:26.230
Really? That seems perfect for you.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.170
I do like dictionaries,

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:32.140
but unlike my sales job where I was
often out by myself,

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:35.910
creating a dictionary requires everyone
to work together.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:38.240
What's the problem with that?

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.350
To be frank, I'm worried I won't be able
to fit in with the rest of the department.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:48.920
Also, I'm afraid that I'm just going to let
everybody down...

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:53.190
Honestly, having such childish worries
at your age...

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:55.590
Is it strange?

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:59.870
No. It's very like you.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:04.000
You just want to get along with everyone
at work.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:07.110
You want to get along and create
a good dictionary.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:08.770
How did you know?

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.180
Because

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:13.610
you and I are bosom buddies.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:16.280
Bosom buddies...

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:19.750
Bosom buddies...

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:23.020
bosom buddy

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:24.820
Just be yourself.

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:28.130
If someone asks you to change
a light bulb, you will.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:29.730
Well, of course.

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:33.800
If I invite you for dinner,
you take me up on my offer.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:35.400
Just keep doing that.

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:38.670
It's all about give and take.

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:41.810
Not just with me,
but with your coworkers too.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:45.880
If you work hard to express
your feelings to them,

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:48.850
everyone will work hard
to accommodate them.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.280
That's how the world works.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.690
The bath is free.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.030
Oh, thank you.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:06.770
You should take a long, relaxing bath
once in a while.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.000
Okay.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.800
You can't relax with just morning showers.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:14.370
Okay.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:18.940
Well, I'm heading for bed.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.050
Thank you for washing the dishes.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.320
No problem. Good night.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:29.820
Tora-san...

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:40.330
Wanting to express myself.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.470
Kojien

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:43.900
Wanting to have a deep understanding
of others.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:47.810
Finding the perfect words.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:52.280
What I can do for Daitokai...

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.960
Yes, hello?

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.330
Oh, what is it?

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:14.100
What? Now?!

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:18.140
Well, I suppose that's okay, but...

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:22.480
Okay.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.740
I'll be in bed.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.880
Okay. Okay. Be careful.

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:32.920
Okay. Bye, then.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:52.770
Tora-san?

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:39.550
Tora-san?

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:51.430
There you are.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:54.530
I'm here to get you.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:21.930
Oh, that's so nice of you.

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.460
You came to get me?

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:28.000
Houchaku: encounter

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:32.400
"I came across hardship after
wandering from place to place"

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:47.090
Hey, it’s a full moon tonight

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:58.100
I speak to you softly as you sleep

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:09.940
Your gestures, attitude, and way of life

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:16.650
Mean so much to me more than any word

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:22.490
So please stay the way you are

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:28.590
I & I = You & I
Feel it

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:34.070
Here with you, forever by your side

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:46.980
I’ve loved you from the day we met

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:58.360
He doesn't like preserved eggs.

00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:01.560
This piece is too greasy,
so not good for his health...

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:05.060
Now it's perfect! I made a plate for you.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:06.200
He can be dedicated.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.430
It's my pleasure!
